.TH HOSTNAME 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
hostname \- show or set the host's name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hostname
.br
.B hostname
.I name
.br
.B hostname \-F
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments,
.B hostname
prints the host's name. Until one is set, it is the first network card's
address (in bus order), the address
.BR rsh (1)
finds the computer by anyway; with no network card,
.BR localhost .
.PP
With a
.IR name ,
it sets the name and keeps it in
.BR /etc/hostname ,
so the computer has it again after a reboot (the boot's
.B /etc/rc
reads that file). An empty
.I name
unsets it.
.B rsh
finds a computer by its name, as well as by its card's address.
.PP
A name is letters, digits,
.B \-
and
.BR . ,
at most 64 of them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-F " file"
Sets the name from the first line of
.IR file ,
keeping nothing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the name was printed or set; 1 for a name that isn't one, or a file
that cannot be read or written.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
hostname
hostname lab
rsh lab ls
.fi
.SH NOTES
GNU
.BR hostname 's
.I name
sets it only until the next boot; keeping it is
.BR "hostnamectl set-hostname" 's
job.
mcos's
.B hostname
does both.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rsh (1),
.BR rshd (1)
